<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tughril</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 990–<a href="/years/1063">1063</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Tughril Beg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tughril, or Tughril Beg, was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>. Leading Turkic Oghuz forces with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg">Chaghri</a>, he defeated the Ghaznavids at Dandanaqan in 1040 and established Seljuk rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In 1055 he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and was recognised as sultan by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, creating a model of sultanic power under caliphal legitimacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tughril.jpg" alt="Tughril" width="298" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1314 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seljuk_Sultan_Tugrul_Bey_sitting_on_his_throne._Topkap%C4%B1_Saray%C4%B1_M%C3%BCzesi_1653-3_(304_B).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tughril was born most likely in the Central Asian steppes, where nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a> roamed in search of pasture. His name was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> word for a bird of prey, possibly the crested goshawk. After the death of his father Mikail, he and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg">Chaghri</a> were reportedly raised by their grandfather Seljuk, the eponymous founder of the dynasty, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jand">Jand</a>. The family seems to have converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, at least nominally, in this period. For decades the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> for warring factions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> in exchange for pasture for their herds.</p><p>They then asked Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> for leave to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, where pastures were scarce for them in their old lands. Mahmud allowed it, and they crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and settled around Sarakhs, Farava and Bavard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Mahmud's successor Masʽud I the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> fought near Nasa in 1034, and Tughril took the city. In 1037 the Seljuk notables declared him sultan and a sermon was delivered in his name. His victory over the Ghaznavids at Dandanaqan in 1040 ended their rule in Greater Khorasan, and he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> his capital. He and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg">Chaghri Beg</a>, whose names were read together in the sermons and struck on coins, shared rule according to the Turkic custom of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> and a yabghu.</p><p>With the help of his half-brother Ibrahim Inal, Tughril conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> and Iraq-i Ajam between 1040 and 1054 and ended Buyid rule in those areas. He took Ray, later making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> his capital, and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> under his control. From 1048 Turcomans under Ibrahim began systematic campaigns into Byzantine lands. In 1055, at the invitation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Qa'im, he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a> by arresting its last ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, Malik al-Rahim. He later put down a revolt by Ibrahim Inal and defeated Arslan al-Basasiri, who had Fatimid backing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tughril died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> in 455 AH (1063), without an heir, near Ray, and was buried there in the Tughril Tower. He was known as devout, built many mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> and, with his vizier Amid al-Mulk Kunduri, restricted other Islamic sects. Some sources describe him as a just ruler who avoided bloodshed, while others report oppressive conduct by him and his army.</p><p>His legacy was an administrative model that joined Turkic political tradition, regional customs and the religious legitimacy of the Abbasid caliphate. It made the sultanate a form of absolute rule that recognised the caliph's spiritual leadership but overshadowed his worldly power. Before the Seljuks Iran had been divided among warring local powers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakuyids">Kakuyids</a> and Ghaznavids, and under Tughril peace and prosperity came to Iran and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tughril I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In December 1055 <b>Tughril Beg</b> of the Seljuk Empire entered Baghdad, was recognised as sultan by the caliph al-Qa'im, and imprisoned the last Buyid emir of Iraq, al-Malik al-Rahim.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1030s Turkmen tribes led by the Seljuk brothers <b>Tughril</b> and Chaghri crossed the Oxus into Khurasan, where the taxation of Mas'ud's officials had alienated the towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Seljuk's grandsons <b>Tughril</b> and Chaghri led their tribesmen into Khorasan in the 1030s, taking Merv and Nishapur in 1037–1038 and throwing off Ghaznavid authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A Byzantine-Georgian army met a Seljuk raid into Byzantine-ruled Armenia, led by Ibrahim Inal, half-brother of Sultan <b>Tughril</b> I, at Kapetron.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-18">18 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ala al-Dawla's son Faramurz surrendered Isfahan to the Seljuk sultan <b>Tughril</b> in 1051 and received Yazd and Abarquh instead.</blockquote><a href="/state/kakuyid-dynasty">Kakuyid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Wahsudan submitted to the Seljuk Turks under <b>Tughril Beg</b> in 1054, and the dynasty became Seljuk vassals.</blockquote><a href="/state/rawwadid-emirate">Rawwadid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Tajrish, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.81,51.43&amp;year=1037" aria-label="Tajrish on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5848 871 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Tughril"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6298 1141h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6298 1141h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.81,51.43&amp;year=1037">What was here in 1037? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
